WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.328
Here are three lines from a Java program.

00:00:03.328 --> 00:00:06.135
The first prints the range of an int

00:00:06.209 --> 00:00:09.016
from about -2.1 billion

00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:11.748
to about +2.1 billion.

00:00:11.822 --> 00:00:14.260
The second stores the largest int

00:00:14.333 --> 00:00:16.180
in a variable called big.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.716
And the third line prints the

00:00:18.792 --> 00:00:20.450
value of big + 1.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.174
What does it print?

00:00:22.174 --> 00:00:24.200
Pause the video and decide.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.085
Before we answer

00:00:29.085 --> 00:00:32.001
why does this course use Java at all?

00:00:32.078 --> 00:00:35.071
You will implement every data structure

00:00:35.147 --> 00:00:37.833
yourself and run it on real inputs.

00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:41.210
Java compiles a program once into bytecode

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.280
and that same bytecode runs on Windows

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.585
macOS and Linux.

00:00:45.585 --> 00:00:47.580
Write once, run anywhere.

00:00:47.930 --> 00:00:49.670
Compared with C and C++

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.644
Java has references instead of pointers

00:00:52.644 --> 00:00:55.545
you can copy a reference and follow it

00:00:55.545 --> 00:00:58.155
but you cannot do arithmetic on it.

00:00:58.155 --> 00:01:00.403
Every array access is checked

00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:03.449
and a garbage collector frees the objects

00:01:03.522 --> 00:01:05.770
that nobody refers to any more.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:08.564
And Java's library already has the

00:01:08.635 --> 00:01:10.791
structures this course builds

00:01:10.791 --> 00:01:13.019
such as ArrayList and HashMap.

00:01:13.019 --> 00:01:15.463
Once you have built one yourself

00:01:15.535 --> 00:01:17.691
you will know what the library

00:01:17.763 --> 00:01:19.200
version does inside.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:21.974
Three names come with Java.

00:01:21.974 --> 00:01:24.818
The JVM, the Java Virtual Machine

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:27.174
is an abstract computer that

00:01:27.256 --> 00:01:28.718
executes bytecode.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.750
The java command on your

00:01:30.831 --> 00:01:32.700
computer implements it.

00:01:33.050 --> 00:01:35.703
The JRE, the Java Runtime Environment

00:01:35.703 --> 00:01:38.147
is what you need to run a program

00:01:38.147 --> 00:01:41.010
a JVM plus the standard class libraries.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:44.484
The JDK, the Java Development Kit

00:01:44.484 --> 00:01:47.792
is what you need to write programs

00:01:47.792 --> 00:01:51.100
the JRE plus the development tools

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.040
above all the compiler, javac.

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:57.559
Notice that Java is platform-independent

00:01:57.559 --> 00:01:59.105
but the JVM is not.

00:01:59.105 --> 00:02:02.351
The bytecode is the same on every system

00:02:02.351 --> 00:02:04.902
and each system has its own JVM

00:02:04.902 --> 00:02:08.225
which translates it for its own processor.

00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:11.703
Since Java 11, you simply install a JDK

00:02:11.703 --> 00:02:13.790
which includes everything.

00:02:14.370 --> 00:02:16.323
Here is the first program.

00:02:16.398 --> 00:02:18.577
public class Welcome declares

00:02:18.652 --> 00:02:20.305
a class named Welcome.

00:02:20.305 --> 00:02:23.235
In Java all code lives inside classes

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:25.714
and a public class must be saved

00:02:25.789 --> 00:02:27.818
in a file of the same name

00:02:27.818 --> 00:02:29.170
Welcome.java.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:32.072
This line declares the main method

00:02:32.072 --> 00:02:33.312
the entry point

00:02:33.312 --> 00:02:36.374
the JVM starts the program by calling it.

00:02:36.447 --> 00:02:38.562
System.out.println

00:02:38.635 --> 00:02:41.843
prints its argument, followed by a new line

00:02:41.843 --> 00:02:44.760
so the program prints: Welcome to Java!

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.765
Every statement ends with a semicolon

00:02:47.765 --> 00:02:50.630
and braces group statements into blocks.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.913
One warning: Java is case-sensitive.

00:02:53.913 --> 00:02:57.335
The slide writes Public with a capital P.

00:02:57.335 --> 00:02:59.209
That is not a keyword

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:01.897
so the program does not compile.

00:03:01.897 --> 00:03:04.260
Keywords are all lower case.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.987
Running a Java program takes two steps.

00:03:08.067 --> 00:03:10.085
You write the source code

00:03:10.165 --> 00:03:11.779
in Welcome.java.

00:03:11.779 --> 00:03:13.554
The compiler, javac

00:03:13.554 --> 00:03:15.894
translates it into bytecode

00:03:15.894 --> 00:03:18.960
which it stores in Welcome.class.

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.377
Then the java command starts a JVM

00:03:22.464 --> 00:03:24.480
which loads Welcome.class

00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:26.407
and calls main

00:03:26.407 --> 00:03:28.510
and the output appears.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.013
If the compiler finds an error

00:03:31.082 --> 00:03:32.957
such as a missing semicolon

00:03:33.026 --> 00:03:34.554
or a misspelt keyword

00:03:34.623 --> 00:03:36.776
it reports a compile-time error

00:03:36.845 --> 00:03:38.581
and writes no class file.

00:03:38.581 --> 00:03:41.220
You go back to the source and fix it.

00:03:41.570 --> 00:03:44.538
If the program compiles but crashes

00:03:44.538 --> 00:03:46.846
or prints the wrong result

00:03:46.846 --> 00:03:50.473
that is a run-time error, or a logic error.

00:03:50.473 --> 00:03:53.606
Again you edit the source, recompile

00:03:53.606 --> 00:03:55.090
and run it again.

00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:59.137
Words such as class, public, static, int

00:03:59.137 --> 00:04:01.759
if, new and this are keywords

00:04:01.759 --> 00:04:05.395
they have a fixed meaning to the compiler

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.763
and a keyword cannot be the

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:11.230
name of a variable, a method or a class.

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:14.046
This program tries to call

00:04:14.141 --> 00:04:16.322
a String variable this.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:19.452
The compiler reports two errors

00:04:19.452 --> 00:04:23.340
not a statement, and semicolon expected.

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:25.679
The messages are confusing

00:04:25.752 --> 00:04:27.667
because the compiler never

00:04:27.741 --> 00:04:29.288
reads this as a name.

00:04:29.288 --> 00:04:32.086
When an error message makes no sense

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.959
look for a keyword or a missing symbol

00:04:35.032 --> 00:04:37.610
just before the place it points to.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.352
A variable of a primitive type

00:04:40.424 --> 00:04:42.082
holds the value itself

00:04:42.082 --> 00:04:44.676
and Java has eight primitive types.

00:04:44.676 --> 00:04:46.910
A boolean holds true or false.

00:04:47.260 --> 00:04:49.573
Four types hold whole numbers

00:04:49.573 --> 00:04:51.887
and they differ only in size

00:04:51.887 --> 00:04:54.971
a byte has 8 bits, a short 16

00:04:54.971 --> 00:04:58.210
an int 32, and a long 64.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.986
Each wrapper class knows its range

00:05:01.063 --> 00:05:04.995
a byte goes from -128

00:05:05.073 --> 00:05:07.540
to 127.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:10.038
Float and double hold numbers

00:05:10.112 --> 00:05:11.297
with a fraction

00:05:11.371 --> 00:05:14.186
a double has 64 bits and about

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.260
15 significant digits.

00:05:16.334 --> 00:05:18.852
And a char is a 16-bit number

00:05:18.926 --> 00:05:21.370
from 0 to 65000

00:05:21.370 --> 00:05:23.592
535.

00:05:23.592 --> 00:05:25.370
It holds one character.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:27.536
Back to the puzzle.

00:05:27.619 --> 00:05:29.622
Whole numbers are stored

00:05:29.705 --> 00:05:31.374
in two's complement.

00:05:31.374 --> 00:05:32.292
In a byte

00:05:32.375 --> 00:05:35.963
the highest bit counts as -128

00:05:36.047 --> 00:05:38.467
instead of plus.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:40.970
127 is a

00:05:41.054 --> 00:05:43.390
0 followed by seven 1s.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:44.692
Now add 1.

00:05:44.692 --> 00:05:47.151
The carry runs all the way up

00:05:47.151 --> 00:05:49.847
and only the highest bit is left

00:05:49.847 --> 00:05:53.020
that is -128.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.347
An int works the same way

00:05:55.426 --> 00:05:57.086
with 32 bits.

00:05:57.086 --> 00:06:00.406
So big + 1 is the most negative int

00:06:00.406 --> 00:06:01.908
-2000000000

00:06:01.908 --> 00:06:04.517
147000000

00:06:04.517 --> 00:06:07.363
483000

00:06:07.363 --> 00:06:09.340
648.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:11.316
No error and no warning

00:06:11.316 --> 00:06:13.530
integer overflow is silent.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.586
In this course it bites in two places

00:06:16.586 --> 00:06:18.580
summing many large numbers

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:20.644
where you should use a long

00:06:20.644 --> 00:06:23.279
and computing the middle of a range

00:06:23.279 --> 00:06:25.629
where you should write low +

00:06:25.629 --> 00:06:27.480
(high - low) / 2.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.335
Numbers with a fraction have

00:06:30.417 --> 00:06:32.042
a different problem.

00:06:32.042 --> 00:06:34.886
A double stores a binary fraction

00:06:34.886 --> 00:06:38.542
and 0.1 has no exact binary form.

00:06:38.623 --> 00:06:41.305
So 0.1 + 0.2

00:06:41.386 --> 00:06:43.661
prints 0.3,

00:06:43.661 --> 00:06:46.180
then 15 zeros and a 4.

00:06:46.530 --> 00:06:49.692
Compare doubles with a tolerance:

00:06:49.692 --> 00:06:52.471
Math.abs(x - 1)

00:06:52.471 --> 00:06:55.153
< 1e-9.

00:06:55.153 --> 00:06:58.890
Never compare them with ==.

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:00.665
One more idea,

00:07:00.665 --> 00:07:03.653
which the whole course depends on.

00:07:03.653 --> 00:07:06.812
Java's types fall into two families.

00:07:06.897 --> 00:07:09.031
A variable of a primitive

00:07:09.117 --> 00:07:12.190
type holds the value: n holds 7.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:15.092
Every other type, such as String,

00:07:15.092 --> 00:07:17.258
arrays, and every class,

00:07:17.258 --> 00:07:18.882
is a reference type.

00:07:18.882 --> 00:07:21.899
The variable s does not hold the text.

00:07:21.899 --> 00:07:24.374
It holds a reference, an arrow

00:07:24.374 --> 00:07:26.771
to an object stored elsewhere

00:07:26.771 --> 00:07:29.710
in an area of memory called the heap.

00:07:29.710 --> 00:07:32.031
A reference can also be null;

00:07:32.031 --> 00:07:33.500
it points nowhere.

00:07:33.850 --> 00:07:36.747
This explains much of Java's behavior.

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:39.199
Copying an int copies the value,

00:07:39.199 --> 00:07:42.319
but copying a reference copies the arrow,

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:45.290
so both names refer to the same object.

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:46.681
To sum up.

00:07:46.681 --> 00:07:49.356
1: javac compiles your source

00:07:49.437 --> 00:07:50.977
code into bytecode,

00:07:51.058 --> 00:07:53.166
and the JVM on each system

00:07:53.247 --> 00:07:55.273
runs the same class file.

00:07:55.273 --> 00:07:58.192
2: The 8 primitive types hold

00:07:58.273 --> 00:08:00.137
values of a fixed size,

00:08:00.218 --> 00:08:02.326
and integer overflow wraps

00:08:02.407 --> 00:08:04.434
around without any error.

00:08:04.434 --> 00:08:05.001
3:

00:08:05.082 --> 00:08:08.162
Every other variable holds a reference

00:08:08.244 --> 00:08:10.270
to an object on the heap.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.365
Try this yourself.

00:08:12.365 --> 00:08:14.886
A byte holds 100.

00:08:14.886 --> 00:08:18.860
What is in it after b += 50?

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:20.048
In part 2,

00:08:20.048 --> 00:08:22.699
we meet the three kinds of variables

00:08:22.699 --> 00:08:25.770
and the statements and loops that use them.

